# Normalise line endings in the repository.
#
# This tree is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites
# checked-out files to CRLF. For most files that is invisible. For two kinds
# here it is not:
#
#   *.sql  — the migration runner checksums each file to detect "applied then
#            edited" drift. A CRLF checkout changes every checksum and the
#            runner refuses to apply anything, reporting all 46 applied
#            migrations as tampered. (The runner now normalises before hashing
#            too; this keeps the bytes themselves stable.)
#   *.sh   — a shell script with CRLF fails on the AWS box with the classic
#            "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M". deploy/aws/ci-deploy.sh is
#            the production deploy path.
#
# PowerShell scripts stay CRLF: they run on Windows, and the scheduled tasks
# that call them are ASCII/CRLF-sensitive (see CLAUDE.md).
* text=auto

*.sql  text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.jks  binary
*.keystore binary
*.aab  binary

# Shell hooks must keep LF. Git's autocrlf would rewrite the shebang to
# "#!/bin/sh\r", which sh rejects -- the pre-push guard would silently stop
# running on every fresh clone, which is exactly when it is needed most.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
